WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:06.780
Any learning activity, reviewing activity,
should be easy, as easy as possible.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:11.040
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here and
today, I want to talk about flashcards.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:16.770
Uh, remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, uh, click

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.150
on the bell for notifications.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.590
And if you follow me on a podcast
service, please leave a comment.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:26.925
So flashcards uh, activities.

00:00:26.955 --> 00:00:27.985
We have them on LingQ.

00:00:28.215 --> 00:00:32.325
It can be simply, you know, you see a
word and you gotta remember the meaning

00:00:32.475 --> 00:00:34.755
of that word, say a target language word.

00:00:34.935 --> 00:00:36.405
What is the meaning in your language?

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:42.045
Or you can have, um, clozed tests where
you fill in the blank with a word.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.620
Or multiple choice.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.820
There are different kinds of
activities that can be forms,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.769
ways of reviewing vocabulary.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.910
And I'm gonna tell you what I think
of these and how I think they, these

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.030
different ways of reviewing, reviewing
vocabulary are most effective.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:04.625
A lot of people use en key or some other,
uh, spaced repetition system where uh,

00:01:04.754 --> 00:01:08.355
you review words and depending on whether
you get it right with the flashcard,

00:01:08.355 --> 00:01:12.405
like you see the word and you don't get
it right, then it generates sort of a

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.525
response within this, uh, algorithm.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:21.884
So that new words are put in front
of you, uh, based on, you know, some

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:26.505
assumptions or some evidence on how
quickly we forget things and how

00:01:26.505 --> 00:01:28.425
often we need to see things again.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:29.605
And so forth and so on.

00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:35.625
So right up front let me say that I have
never been a user of Anki and I'm not a

00:01:35.625 --> 00:01:41.025
big user of flashcards at LingQ, but I use
them to some extent, and I'm gonna tell

00:01:41.025 --> 00:01:46.485
you Steve's approach to flashcards and to
some extent lists and what I think is the

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:49.155
most effective way to review vocabulary.

00:01:49.455 --> 00:01:55.425
Now, remember most of our vocabulary we
pick it up in, in reading and listening.

00:01:55.425 --> 00:02:02.610
We see it in an actual live meaningful uh,
context or we hear it after having read

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.289
it, or we read it after having heard it.

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:08.070
And so gradually these
words start to stick.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.100
It's not a matter in my
opinion of memorization.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:15.690
However, some deliberate
review of words can be helpful.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.370
Here is what I think are the
sort of basic rules of useful

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.430
flashcard or vocabulary review.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:29.730
Number one: do it as soon as possible
after you have come across the word.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:35.460
So in my own case, uh, and here I'm gonna
mention LingQ because, and some people

00:02:35.465 --> 00:02:37.170
don't like it, but that's where I study.

00:02:37.175 --> 00:02:41.220
A lot of people use, uh, LingQ and that's
what I refer to when I talk about my

00:02:41.225 --> 00:02:42.540
own experience of language learning.

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:47.790
So when I'm in LingQ, especially
now in Persian, I use the sentence

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:53.820
mode, um, particularly if, if the
content has come from YouTube, where

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.859
there is very accurate time stamping.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.150
So I deal with a sentence 8, 10, 12 words.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:05.760
And within those 8, 10, 12 words,
there might be three or two or

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.040
one or four that are yellow.

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:10.829
In other words, I've seen them before,
but I still don't know what they mean.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:15.540
Or there might be one or two blue words
that I'm seeing for the first time.

00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:22.695
So what I do is that sentence, when
I finish that sentence, I will do

00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:28.425
a review, usually either in a list
or some form of flashcard, which I

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:33.255
will get to, but the most effective
time to do that review is immediately

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.895
after I've seen the word in a context.

00:03:36.405 --> 00:03:37.065
That's point number one.

00:03:37.785 --> 00:03:43.105
Point number two: in my opinion, we
should never do more than a handful,

00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:49.515
max 10 flashcards at a sitting because
after a while, we're no longer learning

00:03:49.515 --> 00:03:51.495
anything it's becoming tedious.

00:03:51.675 --> 00:03:56.625
We're more and more divorced
from meaningful content

00:03:56.625 --> 00:03:58.185
from a meaningful context.

00:03:58.515 --> 00:04:03.525
So typically if I'm in sentence
mode and I've got 6, 8, 10 words

00:04:03.525 --> 00:04:05.750
to review,  that's very effective.

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.910
I've just seen them in
a meaningful context.

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.650
Some of those words are new.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.320
Some are words that I'm
starting to get to know.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.920
Some of them, I decide I already know
this word and I move it to known,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.380
but I'm dealing with a handful of
words so immediately, or as soon

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.720
as possible after coming across the
word in some content or context.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.000
Second of all, not too many.

00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:32.630
Max 10, preferably 4, 5, 6.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:39.239
Third: any learning activity,
reviewing activity should be

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.549
easy, as easy as possible.

00:04:41.609 --> 00:04:43.469
No scratching your brain.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:45.210
No trying to remember.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:50.460
So obviously a list which has it
all there is a very quick review.

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:55.989
Uh, I use lists for example, to
move the status of a word to known,

00:04:57.255 --> 00:04:59.385
but it's also a very quick review.

00:05:00.105 --> 00:05:04.605
What I find quite effective now is
these new matching pairs that we have

00:05:04.605 --> 00:05:10.575
at LingQ where you are presented with
a total of three words, target language

00:05:11.535 --> 00:05:15.435
and your language or the language you
know, and you just have to connect them.

00:05:15.435 --> 00:05:17.295
And if you go to the
wrong one, it goes red.

00:05:17.295 --> 00:05:20.535
And if you go to the right one,
it's blue, it's very easy to do.

00:05:20.655 --> 00:05:21.645
It's a good review.

00:05:21.675 --> 00:05:23.905
You get the text to speech of the word.

00:05:24.285 --> 00:05:25.275
And so I do three.

00:05:25.545 --> 00:05:28.635
And then if there's another three,
I do another three and then I

00:05:28.635 --> 00:05:30.915
do another three, but it's easy.

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:32.505
I'm not scratching my brain.

00:05:32.835 --> 00:05:36.075
All the time spent scratching
your brain trying to remember

00:05:36.075 --> 00:05:38.895
something to me is not effective.

00:05:39.225 --> 00:05:45.885
It's more a matter of exposing yourself
to this vocabulary again with a minimum

00:05:45.885 --> 00:05:48.435
amount of effort as quickly as possible.

00:05:48.465 --> 00:05:54.150
So as soon as soon after having come
across it in a particular source of

00:05:54.150 --> 00:06:01.559
content, such as a sentence in our
sentence view at, at LingQ, uh, easy.

00:06:03.059 --> 00:06:03.880
And what else?

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:04.570
And not too many.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:05.380
Okay.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:06.870
So those are the conditions.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.570
And on that basis, it's very effective.

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:13.140
Each sentence, you do a
sentence, you review the words

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.280
that came out of that sentence.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.010
Some new, some words you're
still trying to learn, and then

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.150
you move on tothe next sentence.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:21.240
So you stay in the content item.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.760
You don't lose your thread
in the content item.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.810
Uh, it's not like, uh, reviewing
hundreds of words in flashcards where

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:30.289
you're now divorced from any content.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:35.740
And I have found that not only is it
tedious to do that, but, uh, as I've

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:41.830
said before, even in Persian, I have
30,000 words that I have saved and

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.659
I only know 10,000 of those words.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:47.050
And so I have lots of words that
I'm in the process of learning.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:52.120
If I were to review all of those in
some kind of a space re repetition

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:56.200
algorithm, I would spend a lot
of my time just reviewing words.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.885
I prefer to stay in meaningful content.

00:07:01.245 --> 00:07:07.455
Now another form of review,
which is very effective is

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.525
where that review has a purpose.

00:07:10.005 --> 00:07:14.174
Part of the problem with
reviewing, you know, long lists

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.375
of flashcards is I never am that
confident that I'm really learning.

00:07:19.875 --> 00:07:23.925
um, I'm reviewing, scratching my
head, whatever, but I'm not really

00:07:23.925 --> 00:07:27.645
sure that anything is sticking
and I'm divorced from the content.

00:07:28.005 --> 00:07:34.065
However, if I review at LingQ my
status three words, so I go to

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:39.285
my vocab section and I filter for
just those words that are familiar.

00:07:39.765 --> 00:07:43.395
We have three statuses, new, kind
of starting to get to know it,

00:07:43.395 --> 00:07:45.645
and now it's kind of familiar,
but you're not totally sure.

00:07:46.105 --> 00:07:48.249
So I have lots of these words in LingQ.

00:07:48.270 --> 00:07:52.020
If I look at my vocab section, I have
each, I, I don't know if there's 25

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:57.180
or how many words there are in a list
uh, but I have like 45 pages of these.

00:07:57.210 --> 00:08:00.719
So I have a lot of words that
are status three, familiar.

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:06.120
If I go to that list and start
going down them, I find a lot of

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.400
words that I actually know now.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.530
So therefore I have a sense of purpose.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:15.690
I am actually increasing my known words
total while I'm going through this list.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.540
And of course, as I'm going through
them, I'm looking at the other words

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.880
that I'm not yet totally confident about.

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:27.135
And I will find x, whether 20, 25% of
these words that I can move to known.

00:08:27.195 --> 00:08:32.294
So this improves my score, my known
words total, uh, keeps my streak going.

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:34.395
So I feel as if I'm achieving something.

00:08:34.635 --> 00:08:38.205
So I think that's the fourth element,
rather than just sitting there and

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:41.565
going through flashcards in the hope
that some of that is gonna stick in

00:08:41.565 --> 00:08:44.925
the hope that the algorithm is somehow
gonna help you learn these words,

00:08:44.985 --> 00:08:47.665
which I have no confidence is the case.

00:08:48.015 --> 00:08:49.620
Other people do they swear by them.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:50.250
That's fine.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:54.300
We can all do what we like to do, but I'm
talking about my approach to flashcards.

00:08:54.660 --> 00:09:02.610
I prefer to stay with the content one
sentence at a time, review the words

00:09:02.699 --> 00:09:05.819
right after the sentence, as close
as possible to when I've, you know,

00:09:05.819 --> 00:09:07.199
been able to hear it and read it.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:13.349
Uh, I want the activity and I
want no more than say six to 10.

00:09:14.339 --> 00:09:18.285
I want the, the review activity
to be as easy as possible, either

00:09:18.285 --> 00:09:21.344
a list or, uh, matching pairs.

00:09:21.824 --> 00:09:25.964
And finally, if I am gonna deal with a
longer list, then I wanna have the sense

00:09:26.055 --> 00:09:27.765
that I'm actually achieving something.

00:09:27.944 --> 00:09:31.785
I am adding to my known words,
total, I'm maintaining my streak.

00:09:32.604 --> 00:09:37.245
I'm, you know, generating the number of
coins that I want to generate for that.

00:09:37.965 --> 00:09:43.635
So on all, uh, for all of those reasons
and in that way, I enjoy doing flashcards.

00:09:44.055 --> 00:09:48.525
So I know many people have
different approaches, which is fine.

00:09:48.525 --> 00:09:51.015
I look forward to your comments.

00:09:51.525 --> 00:09:52.395
Thank you for listening.

00:09:52.995 --> 00:09:53.595
Bye for now.

00:09:53.595 --> 00:09:58.845
So I will use Catalan, which is not
a language that I know and show you

00:09:58.845 --> 00:10:01.095
how I would attack this new language.

00:10:01.095 --> 00:10:03.405
So this happens to be a
sentence out of a mini story.

00:10:04.185 --> 00:10:11.895
So because the mini stories are
timestamped I can hear this sentence

00:10:12.555 --> 00:10:14.925
...
so I can hear listen
to it again if I want.

00:10:15.345 --> 00:10:18.735
Now here I have some yellow words
that I've seen before, and I have

00:10:18.735 --> 00:10:20.175
some blue words that are new.

00:10:20.595 --> 00:10:26.785
So I might look at, I might,
first of all, do that ... okay.

00:10:30.395 --> 00:10:35.035
So ... baby ... will be.

00:10:35.055 --> 00:10:45.495
And I presume this is subjective content,
... with ... so now I will, because my rules

00:10:45.495 --> 00:10:52.755
for flashcards are right away after having
encountered the word, uh, easy and few.

00:10:52.995 --> 00:10:54.135
So here I have five.

00:10:54.194 --> 00:10:55.665
So I study the sentence.

00:10:56.915 --> 00:10:59.325
...is a baby ... okay.

00:11:00.210 --> 00:11:14.540
He, she is, uh, ... in other words, ...so
it looks like the E is like ... they

00:11:16.349 --> 00:11:22.079
um, with, okay, now I don't do this.

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:24.900
We have this, uh, dictation thing,
which chance to your pronunciation,

00:11:25.199 --> 00:11:31.995
this all comes out of a, sort of
recent, uh, version of the app,

00:11:32.025 --> 00:11:39.645
which soon will become available
to you on iOS and on, um, Android.

00:11:40.235 --> 00:11:44.825
Uh, if I go to my, uh, where are you?

00:11:44.895 --> 00:11:50.355
Persian And, uh, familiar,
I have a lot of words.

00:11:50.505 --> 00:11:56.805
I have 40 pages worth and I don't
know how many items there are in here.

00:11:56.805 --> 00:12:06.595
And I will find words in there
that ... I might give myself credit for

00:12:07.615 --> 00:12:10.695
that ... um, so maybe I'll take that.

00:12:11.145 --> 00:12:12.765
And then I work myself down.

00:12:14.055 --> 00:12:17.785
... this comes up all the time now with
the Iranians and  drones and stuff

00:12:18.375 --> 00:12:22.665
and, and I will normally find a lot of
words there that I can move to known.

00:12:22.665 --> 00:12:26.295
So there I do my review in a
list, but I do it with a purpose.

00:12:26.415 --> 00:12:28.364
The purpose is moving words to known.

00:12:28.935 --> 00:12:33.074
So there you have it a quick, uh,
review of how I use flashcards.

